<!-- Website teaching layer for android/architecture/mvvm-vs-mvi.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against developer.android.com (UI layer, UI events, Compose strong skipping) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A checkout monitoring collector records "SAVE10 applied" with the old total of $100.00, followed by $90.00. The final screen looks right, but the intermediate combination has already reached another observer.</p>

The app never computed a wrong price. It exposed the promo code and the total as two separate streams and wrote them one after the other, so a collector could see the new code with the old total. The difference between MVVM and MVI is mostly about that gap: whether a screen's state is several values that must be kept consistent by hand, or one value that changes all at once. This chapter shows where each pattern earns its cost.

<!-- @after The Problem with MVVM at Scale :: animation -->
### Watch independently updated values become inconsistent

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Sequential writes vs one atomic update</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">MVVM: three flows</span><div class="node" data-el="mPromo" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>promoCode</code></strong><small data-text="mPromoV">""</small></div><div class="node" data-el="mTotal" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>totalPrice</code></strong><small data-text="mTotalV">100.0</small></div><div class="node" data-el="mLoad" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>isLoading</code></strong><small data-text="mLoadV">false</small></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="mUi" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>A collector can observe</strong><small data-text="mUiV">$100.00</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">MVI: one state</span><div class="node" data-el="iState" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>CheckoutState</code></strong><small data-text="iStateV">promo "", total 100.0, loading false</small></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="iUi" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>A collector can observe</strong><small data-text="iUiV">$100.00</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Start",
"caption": "Both screens begin consistent: no promo code, total 100.0.",
"add": [
"mPromo",
"mTotal",
"mLoad",
"mUi",
"iState",
"iUi"
],
"state": {
"mUi": "done",
"iUi": "done"
}
},
{
"label": "Apply SAVE10",
"caption": "Both set loading. In MVVM that is one write to one flow; in MVI it is one update to the whole state.",
"state": {
"mLoad": "active",
"iState": "active"
},
"text": {
"mLoadV": "true",
"iStateV": "promo \"\", total 100.0, loading true",
"mUiV": "spinner",
"iUiV": "spinner"
}
},
{
"label": "Response: write the promo",
"caption": "MVVM writes promoCode first. A collector on another execution context can observe the new code with the old total. Two adjacent writes on Main do not, by themselves, demonstrate a rendered frame between them.",
"state": {
"mPromo": "active",
"mLoad": "",
"mUi": "error"
},
"text": {
"mPromoV": "\"SAVE10\"",
"mUiV": "promo SAVE10, total 100.0, loading true"
}
},
{
"label": "Write the total",
"caption": "The next write updates the total, while loading is still true. Separate streams do not make the combination atomic for every collector.",
"state": {
"mTotal": "active",
"mPromo": "done",
"mUi": "warn"
},
"text": {
"mTotalV": "90.0",
"mUiV": "promo SAVE10, total 90.0, loading true"
}
},
{
"label": "Clear loading",
"caption": "MVVM needed three writes to reach a consistent state. MVI made one update with copy(promoCode, totalPrice, isLoading) in a single atomic replacement of the state value.",
"state": {
"mLoad": "done",
"mTotal": "done",
"mUi": "done",
"iState": "done",
"iUi": "done"
},
"text": {
"mLoadV": "false",
"mUiV": "SAVE10 applied · $90.00",
"iStateV": "promo \"SAVE10\", total 90.0, loading false",
"iUiV": "SAVE10 applied · $90.00"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>Collectors of one <code>StateFlow</code> see either the old complete state or the new complete state, never a mix.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after The MVI Pattern :: visual -->
### The MVI loop

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>UI</strong><small>renders <code>CheckoutState</code>; sends <code>CheckoutIntent</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>processIntent()</code></strong><small>does async work outside the reducer</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Reducer / <code>update { copy(…) }</code></strong><small>the only place state changes; pure</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong><code>StateFlow&lt;CheckoutState&gt;</code></strong><small>one value, emitted to the UI</small></div></div><figcaption>Every change passes through one function, so logging, replay and tests attach in one place.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after UI Events — State First, Best-Effort Effects by Exception :: visual -->
### Where should this action live?

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="node is-key"><strong>Did the action start in the UI and stay there?</strong><small>e.g. a tap that opens help</small></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Yes → handle it in the UI</strong><small>no ViewModel round trip</small></div><div class="node"><strong>No → the ViewModel produced it</strong><small>e.g. "payment confirmed, leave this screen"</small></div></div><div class="down"></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Losing it would be wrong → state</strong><small>put it in UI state; the UI acts on it, then reports it consumed</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Losing it is acceptable → best-effort effect</strong><small>a <code>Channel</code> for process-local effects, accepting that one can be dropped</small></div></div></div><figcaption>The state-first branch follows Android's UI events guidance. The best-effort Channel branch is the chapter's explicit policy for effects whose loss is acceptable, not an Android recommendation.</figcaption></figure>

