<!-- @after Decision: When to Use What :: check -->
**MVVM or MVI?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>A settings screen with six independent toggles, each saved immediately.</summary>

MVVM. No two values must agree with each other, so an intent and reducer add ceremony without preventing any bug.
</details>

<details><summary>A video player whose play state, position, buffering and selected quality all change asynchronously.</summary>

MVI. Several fields must agree while async events race, and a single state value makes each transition atomic and testable.
</details>

<details><summary>A profile form with name, email and a "Save" button enabled only when both are valid.</summary>

Either works. One state data class with a derived `canSave` keeps the rule in one place; a full intent hierarchy is optional.
</details>

<!-- @before The Problem with MVVM at Scale :: review -->
